WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.480
I wanted to see inside a developing zebrafish. These

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.360
photographs are from an experiment that did not work.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:20.440
The specimens were tiny. A small change in orientation could decide whether

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:28.280
a section passed through the anatomy I needed, or missed it entirely.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:36.440
My plan was to hold each specimen in a two percent

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:44.280
agarose matrix. That support would establish orientation before subsequent tissue processing.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:52.930
After dehydration and clearing, paraffin was meant to provide sufficient mechanical

00:00:52.930 --> 00:01:01.260
support for microtomy. On paper, each material had a reasonable job.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:08.950
But a hydrophilic gel and a hydrophobic embedding medium do not automatically

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:16.300
form a mechanically coherent block. Their interface was the vulnerable point.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:24.440
The processed block formed distinguishable layers. At the microtome, they separated

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:32.280
and broke apart. A usable ribbon of histological sections never appeared.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:40.440
The failure involved the materials, not simply the machine. But what

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:48.280
I could observe was separation. Its precise physical explanation remained unresolved.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:55.950
The grade and thermal behaviour of agarose might have contributed. I did

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:03.300
not establish that experimentally, and the photographs alone cannot prove it.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:11.440
A future comparison could examine agarose grade, concentration and processing conditions, then

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:19.280
measure block integrity during sectioning. Those are proposals, not completed experiments.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.990
No interpretable section came from this attempt. I kept the

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:30.380
photographs anyway. The failed block was the evidence I had.
